    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we start by rewriting the quadratic terms in a form that makes it easier to identify and potentially minimize them. This is done by completing the square.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x. \\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, dividing by 2 to get 3, and then squaring this:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = x^2 + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 9 - 9 = (x+3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y. \\]\nThis can be factored directly as:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2(y^2 - 2y + 1 - 1) = 2((y-1)^2 - 1) = 2(y-1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow, substituting these completed squares back into the original function, we get:\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 - 9 + 2(y-1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the constants:\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2\\) represents the sum of two squares, and each square is non-negative, so the minimum value of each term is 0. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) occurs when both \\((x+3)^2\\) and \\(2(y-1)^2\\) are 0. This happens when \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) into the function, we get:\n\\[ z = ((-3) + 3)^2 + 2(1 - 1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
